-- title:
--   Mumford frame at r: tame stabilisers, finite quotients, class sets
-- statement:
--   The data are: rationals $a_1,b_1$; a nonzero squarefree $N$; primes $q,q'$ with $q'\neq q$, neither dividing $N$, both at least $5$; primes $r,\bar r$ with $\bar r\neq r$, neither dividing $N$, both at least $5$; the hypothesis that $a_1<0$, $b_1<0$ and that for a height-one prime $v$ of $\mathcal O_{\mathbb Q}$ every nonzero element of $\mathbb H[\mathbb Q,a_1,b_1]\otimes_{\mathbb Q}\mathbb Q_v$ is a unit precisely when $\bar r\in v$; $\mathbb Z$-submodules $\Lambda_1,R_1$ of $\mathbb H=\mathbb H[\mathbb Q,a_1,b_1]$ with $\Lambda_1$ an order maximal among orders, $R_1$ an intersection of two maximal orders of relative index $N$, and $R_1\le\Lambda_1$; a finite-idèlic unit $n_1$ in the $r$-th prime Hecke set of $R_1$, such that $\mathrm{meetOrder}\,R_1\,n_1=R_1\cap n_1R_1n_1^{-1}$ is an Eichler order of level $Nr$, is fixed by conjugation by $n_1$, and such that shifting twice by $n_1$ is the identity on the class set of its finite-idèle stabiliser; finiteness of that class set and of the class set of the stabiliser of $R_1$; a valuation subring $A_2$ of $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ with $r$ a nonunit, and a height-one prime $v_2$ containing $r$; an injective $\mathbb Q$-algebra map $\iota_2$ from $\mathbb H$ to $2\times 2$ matrices over $K_0=\mathrm{ratClosure}\,A_2$, the closure of the prime subfield in the completion of $A_2$ for its valuation, and the homomorphism $\rho_2:\mathbb H^\times\to\mathrm{PGL}_2(K_0)$ it induces; families $s_2,sf_2$ indexed by the primes $\ell\notin\{q,q'\}$ with $sf_2(\ell)$ localising to $s_2(\ell)\otimes 1$ away from $r$ and to $1$ at places containing $r$, with $\mathrm{diag}(\ell)\,sf_2(\ell)^{-1}$ lying in $\mathrm{levelHeckeUSet}\,\Lambda_1\,(\mathrm{meetOrder}\,R_1\,n_1)\,\ell$ when $\ell\mid N$ and in the $\ell$-th prime Hecke set of $\mathrm{meetOrder}\,R_1\,n_1$ otherwise, and $\mathrm{nrd}(s_2(\ell))=\ell$; a discrete valuation ring $R_0$ with fraction field $K_0$ whose image is exactly the closed unit ball; and an action of $\mathbb H^\times$ on the homothety classes of full $R_0$-lattices in $K_0^2$ given by $\rho_2$, preserving adjacency in the Bruhat–Tits tree. Writing $\Gamma(S)=\mathrm{awayUnits}\,S\,v_2$ for the units of $\mathbb H$ lying in the subgroup generated by the local box units of $S$ at every place other than $v_2$, the conclusion asserts: (i) for every dart $d$ of the tree, the stabiliser of $d$ in $\rho_2(\Gamma(R_1))$ is finite of order not divisible by the residue characteristic of the completion of $A_2$, and likewise for $\rho_2(\Gamma(\mathrm{meetOrder}\,R_1\,(sf_2\ell)))$ for each $\ell\notin\{q,q'\}$; (ii) the quotients of the vertex set and of the dart set of the tree by $\Gamma(R_1)$, and by each $\Gamma(\mathrm{meetOrder}\,R_1\,(sf_2\ell))$, are finite; and (iii) there are bijections $\varepsilon_V$ from the vertex quotient onto the class set of the finite-idèle stabiliser of $R_1$ and $\varepsilon_E$ from the dart quotient onto the class set of the finite-idèle stabiliser of $\mathrm{meetOrder}\,R_1\,n_1$ which intertwine the two source maps of the quotient degeneracy datum (orbit of $d\mapsto d.\mathrm{fst}$, resp. $d.\mathrm{snd}$) with the two maps of $\mathrm{classSetDegeneracyData}\,R_1\,n_1$ (forgetting the level, resp. right multiplication of a representative by $n_1$), and such that for every dart $d$ the order of the stabiliser of $d$ in $\rho_2(\Gamma(R_1))$ equals the weight $\mathrm{classWeight}$ of $\varepsilon_E$ applied to the orbit of $d$, namely the unit weight of the conjugate of $\mathrm{meetOrder}\,R_1\,n_1$ by a representative.
--
--   This is the arithmetic frame for the Čerednik–Drinfel'd/Mumford–Kurihara description of a Shimura curve at one place of bad reduction: the quotient of the Bruhat–Tits tree at $r$ by the group of units of an Eichler order away from $r$ is a finite graph with tame finite dart stabilisers, and this quotient graph, with its degeneracy datum and stabiliser widths, is identified with the class-set graph of the pair $(R_1,n_1)$. It supplies in one step the finiteness, tameness and combinatorial-identification hypotheses used by the constructions of totally degenerate coverings and by the subsequent level-raising and $\Omega$-action arguments.
